# ORCHESTRATOR_SHOWCASE.md â€” addendum for the showcase track (WO-05a, WO-S1 to WO-S4)

`Docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md` applies unchanged: dispatch with `bash Tools/astra.sh`, verify, accept or reject in at most two rounds, commit, worklog, stop conditions, token discipline. This addendum says how to verify each kind of work in this track. `Docs/ORCHESTRATOR_PHASE1.md` (Unity MCP, batch builds, adb) is retired; do not follow it.

## Step 0 additions

- `dotnet --version` must print 8.x or newer; if not, stop and tell Bryan (step 2 of `Docs/SHOWCASE.md`).
- After WO-05a is accepted, generate the JavaScript transcripts the C# port is measured against: for each of `slice_play`, `slice_pull_play`, `slice_autoplan`, `stage1_pm_play`, `stage2_pull_play`, `stage1_noaction` run `node Monitor/src/sim/headless.js Data/Scenarios/<stage>.json --script Monitor/test/scripts/<name>.json --out Data/transcripts/<name>.json` (no `--script` for `stage1_noaction`; the stage is the one the script name starts with). Commit `Data/transcripts/`. Regenerate and recommit whenever the JavaScript simulation changes.
- For the second goal only: run `Tools/import_lobgame_assets.ps1` (PowerShell) yourself before dispatching WO-S3; it copies about 1.7 GB from `..\LOB-Game\Assets` into `Assets\LOBGame\` with the `.meta` files, read-only on the source. Astra never touches LOB-Game. `Assets/LOBGame/` is gitignored; the script is the source of truth.

## Verifying web work (WO-S1)

Playwright against the static server the WO adds (`npm --prefix Monitor run showcase:serve`), never against `file://` (ES modules do not load from files). Open the landing page, start a showcase with the slice and autoplan on, open the Plan and Site tabs in the same browser context, press Start on the host tab, and check the WO's list. Screenshots at 1280Ã—800 and at 390Ã—844 (phone) into `Monitor/out/showcase/`; look at them. After Bryan publishes (his step 4), `curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" https://tamu-game-lab.github.io/lob-over/` is the last check of the first goal; if it is not 200 yet, say so and still print `READY_FOR_PAGES` once WO-S2 is accepted, because publishing is his step.

## Verifying the C# simulation (WO-S2)

`dotnet test Sim/LOBOver.Sim.Tests` from the repo root is the acceptance; read its summary, not its full output. A parity failure is a reject whose note quotes the first failing assertion (which transcript, which day, which field, expected versus actual). Never loosen a tolerance; if a rule is ambiguous in the JavaScript, the JavaScript is the specification.

## Verifying Unity work (WO-S3, WO-S4) without the Unity MCP

1. Compile check, only when the Editor is closed (no `Temp/UnityLockfile` in the project): `"C:\Program Files\Unity\Hub\Editor\6000.3.8f1\Editor\Unity.exe" -batchmode -nographics -quit -projectPath . -logFile Monitor/out/unity-compile.log`, wait for it to exit (up to 15 minutes on the first import of the copied assets), then `grep -c "error CS" Monitor/out/unity-compile.log` must be 0. Any `error CS` line is a reject with that line quoted. If the lockfile exists, skip this and go to 2.
2. Then stop for Bryan with a message of at most six lines: what to open, which menu item to run, what to press, what he should see in the first 60 seconds, and what to reply. His one-line reply is the check's result; record it in the worklog. If he reports something wrong, that is the rejection note's content, in his words plus the WO section it belongs to.
3. Never open, edit or review scene or prefab YAML; Astra builds scenes from editor scripts, and the only acceptable scene is one the menu item regenerates.

## Publishing (second goal, before any Unity work)

One public repository holds everything: `https://github.com/tamu-game-lab/lob-over` (Bryan created it with a blank README). Source lives on `main`, the built site on `gh-pages`, Pages serves `https://tamu-game-lab.github.io/lob-over/`. Steps, in order, all run by you (ops, not feature code; `.claude/settings.json` allows `git push`, `powershell`, `gh`, `dotnet`):

1. `git remote get-url origin || git remote add origin https://github.com/tamu-game-lab/lob-over.git`, then `git fetch origin`.
2. If `git log --oneline origin/main` shows exactly one commit and `git show --stat origin/main` touches only `README.md`, the remote holds nothing of value: `git push --force -u origin main`. Otherwise `git merge --allow-unrelated-histories -X ours origin/main -m "Merge GitHub initial commit"` and `git push -u origin main`. Bryan may have to complete a GitHub sign-in window the first time; wait for it.
3. Local path fix: the showcase test server and e2e assume the old subpath. In `Monitor/src/showcase/serve.js` change `/lob-over-showcase` to `/lob-over`; in `Monitor/e2e/showcase.e2e.js` change the base to `http://localhost:7340/lob-over/`; grep `Monitor/` for any other `lob-over-showcase`; run `npm --prefix Monitor run e2e`; commit "Showcase: one repository, /lob-over path".
4. `Sim/NuGet.Config` now points at nuget.org again (it was cleared for Astra's offline sandbox): run `dotnet restore Sim/LOBOver.Sim.Tests` and `dotnet test Sim/LOBOver.Sim.Tests -c Release` once with network; commit if the lock files changed.
5. Publish: `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File Tools/publish_showcase.ps1` (it builds `Showcase/dist`, pushes it to `gh-pages` through a worktree in `Showcase/.publish`, and prints the URL).
6. Pages: `gh auth status`; if signed in, `gh api --method POST -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" /repos/tamu-game-lab/lob-over/pages -f "source[branch]=gh-pages" -f "source[path]=/"`; a 409 means Pages already exists, then `gh api --method PUT ... /repos/tamu-game-lab/lob-over/pages -f "source[branch]=gh-pages" -f "source[path]=/"`. If `gh` is missing or not signed in, print for Bryan: "GitHub â†’ lob-over â†’ Settings â†’ Pages â†’ Build and deployment â†’ Source: Deploy from a branch â†’ Branch: gh-pages, / (root) â†’ Save" and continue; re-check at the end of the run.
7. `README.md`: add one line under the title, "Play it in the browser: https://tamu-game-lab.github.io/lob-over/ (the field manual PDF is linked on the landing page)"; commit and push.
8. Verify: poll `curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" https://tamu-game-lab.github.io/lob-over/` every 30 s for up to 10 minutes after Pages is enabled; 200 is the check. Then open the live landing page, host, Plan and Site with Playwright once (same checks as WO-S1, against the live URL) and keep the screenshots in `Monitor/out/showcase-live/`. Record the URL and the gh-pages commit in the worklog.

After every accepted Unity work order in this goal, `git push` as well, so the public repository stays current.

## Finish lines

- First goal: `READY_FOR_PAGES` when WO-05a, WO-S1 and WO-S2 are ACCEPTED.
- Second goal: `READY_FOR_HEADSET` when the site answers 200 at its Pages URL, WO-S3 and WO-S4 are ACCEPTED with Bryan's Play-mode check recorded, and `main` is pushed. Build and Run is his step.
# SHOWCASE.md â€” the stakeholder vertical slice

Revised October 20, 2026. This replaces the "VR slice as a client of a local host" track in `Docs/VR_SLICE.md` sections B to E (kept for the study setup later). The showcase has three pieces, each self-contained, none needing a server on a LAN:

1. **A web page on GitHub Pages.** The simulation runs in the browser. One tab is the Plan seat, another the Site seat, a third the facilitator; a phone can join the same run as the Site seat by room code. Plan can run on autopilot, so one presenter can show the whole chain alone. Links to the field manual (PDF) and the design summary sit on the landing page.
2. **The field manual as a PDF**, generated from the scenario (already done by `Paper/make_kit.py`), published with the page.
3. **A Quest 3S app you build yourself with Build and Run.** The simulation is embedded (a C# port proven against the JavaScript one), so the headset needs no PC. Two modes on the start panel: **VR Site**, the inspector seat at full scale, and **MR Diorama**, passthrough with the site as a two-metre model on the table in front of you, the LOB chart floating above it, crews moving, the collapse, the argument, the rain, pointable with the controller. The diorama is the stakeholder mode; the VR seat is the study mode.

The three-minute slice (`Data/Scenarios/slice_texas.json`, measured in `Docs/VR_SLICE.md`) is the default scenario in all three; the two Texas stages remain selectable.

## What you do (numbered, in order)

1. PowerShell in `LOB-OVER`, Unity closed: `git add -A; git commit -m "Showcase plan, slice, work orders"`.
2. `dotnet --version`. If it fails: `winget install Microsoft.DotNet.SDK.8`, reopen PowerShell. (The C# simulation is built and tested with `dotnet`, outside Unity, so the orchestrator can verify it.)
3. Launch and paste the first goal (below). Walk away; it runs WO-05a, WO-S1, WO-S2 with no Unity. Two to four hours.
4. When it prints `READY_FOR_PAGES`: one public repository holds everything, `https://github.com/tamu-game-lab/lob-over` (source on `main`, the built site on `gh-pages`). The second goal connects the local repository to it, pushes `main`, publishes `gh-pages` with `Tools\publish_showcase.ps1`, and turns Pages on through `gh` if it is installed and signed in; otherwise it tells you the three clicks (Settings â†’ Pages â†’ Deploy from a branch â†’ `gh-pages` â†’ `/ (root)`). Be at the PC for the first push: Git may open a GitHub sign-in window. The site is `https://tamu-game-lab.github.io/lob-over/`.
5. Launch the second goal (below). It publishes the site, then copies the LOB-Game art into this project, writes the Unity code and the scene builder, and stops when it needs you. One to two days with your checks in between.
6. When it says "check in Unity": open LOB-OVER in Unity 6000.3.8f1, menu **LOB Over â†’ Build â†’ Site Scene**, open `Assets/LOBOver/Scenes/Site.unity`, press **Play**. Desktop preview: the chain plays by itself (collapse Day 4, argument Day 5, rain Day 9, finish Day 24); WASD and mouse to look. Report what you saw in one line; the run continues.
7. When it prints `READY_FOR_HEADSET`: headset plugged in, developer mode on; File â†’ Build Profiles â†’ Android â†’ **Build and Run**. Put on the headset, pick MR Diorama. That is the stakeholder demo. Pick VR Site for the inspector seat.
8. Each later change to a scenario, the manual or the voice lines: `python Paper\make_kit.py Data\Scenarios\slice_texas.json`, `.venv\Scripts\python Tools\voice\make_voice.py Data\Scenarios\slice_texas.json`, `Tools\publish_showcase.ps1`, and Build and Run again. Nothing else.

## The two goals

First (web and simulation, Unity closed):

```
/goal Read Docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md and Docs/ORCHESTRATOR_SHOWCASE.md and follow them. Dispatch WO-05a, then WO-S1, then WO-S2 through bash Tools/astra.sh; verify each with its acceptance checks; accept or reject (max two rounds); commit each; keep Docs/WORKLOG.md current. Done when all three are ACCEPTED and READY_FOR_PAGES is printed, or a stop condition is reported to me.
```

Second (publish, then Unity; Unity may be open or closed, the orchestrator checks):

```
/goal Read Docs/ORCHESTRATOR.md and Docs/ORCHESTRATOR_SHOWCASE.md and follow them, including the addendum's "Publishing" section: connect origin to https://github.com/tamu-game-lab/lob-over.git, push main, publish gh-pages with Tools/publish_showcase.ps1, enable Pages through gh if available, put the Pages link in README.md, and verify the site answers. Then run Tools/import_lobgame_assets.ps1 and dispatch WO-S3 then WO-S4 through bash Tools/astra.sh; verify what can be verified without Unity, then stop and ask me to check in Unity exactly as the addendum says; accept or reject (max two rounds); commit and push each; keep Docs/WORKLOG.md current. Done when the site is live, both WOs are ACCEPTED and READY_FOR_HEADSET is printed, or a stop condition is reported to me.
```

## What is in and what is out

In: the three seats' views and verbs as designed; the slice and both Texas stages; the no-verdict rule; the manual PDF; the result card; voice placeholders; LOB-Game's machines, workers, pipe, site materials and sounds; passthrough diorama; the inspector seat.

Out, until after the showcase: the Node host for LAN study sessions (still works, unchanged), the paper kit at the table (the PDF stands in), hand tracking, Stage 3 cities, recorded voice, a shared Plan seat on a second PC (the room code covers a phone Site seat; a second Plan device is Phase 2).

## Work orders

| WO | What exists when it is accepted | Verified by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| WO-05a | The simulation fixes already decided (O10, O11, O13) | `npm test`, headless runs |
| WO-S1 | The web showcase: in-browser host, three seats in tabs, room code for a phone, autoplan, landing page, manual PDF, `Showcase/dist/`, publish script | Playwright against a static server; the Pages URL after you publish |
| WO-S2 | The C# simulation as a plain .NET library with xunit parity tests against the JavaScript transcripts, consumed by Unity through an asmdef | `dotnet test` |
| WO-S3 | The site in Unity from LOB-Game's assets, driven by the embedded simulation and an autoplan director; desktop preview with keyboard verbs | compile check; you in Play mode |
| WO-S4 | Start panel with the three modes; VR Site seat; MR Diorama; Android build settings | compile check; you with Build and Run |

