> Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:98:## The VR seat
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:99:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:100:Site is an inspector. The seat is built around noticing, with one authority, because free 
movement and a long verb list are what make VR tiring and what unbalanced the first draft. Site never changes the 
plan: moving a machine, placing protection, holding a crew beyond a safety stop and entering codes are Plan's.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:101:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:102:- **Movement.** A site truck runs on the haul road beside the trench and stops at any station 
stake; the player dismounts to stand at the stake. The cab is a fixed frame of reference, turns are snap turns, and 
there is no free locomotion. Riding is not idle time: an order reaches a crew at once only when Site is standing 
there, so Site chooses where to be.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:103:- **What only Site sees.** The site as it is today: trench walls and water, protection in 
place or missing, machine plates, pipe stencils and damage, delivery tickets, the sky, trucks queued at the gate, the 
permit number posted at each zone board, and crews working, idle or arguing. Held crews get visibly angrier the longer 
they stand, and the daily report never says so. The monitor's actual line is yesterday's feet; Site is live.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:104:- **The clipboard.** Three buttons on the wrist: Flag, Stop work, Confirm. Inspection needs no 
button; looking at an indicator for two seconds reads it out. Controllers first; the four verbs map to hand tracking 
on the Quest 3S later without redesign.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:105:- **Field deviations.** A new event class, one or two per stage, that only Site can catch:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:106:  1. F1, order not received: a crew keeps working after a hold, or keeps digging after a stop. 
An order lands at the next shift unless Site is there.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:107:  2. F2, pushing ahead: excavation crosses a zone boundary without a permit, and the city 
fines it. Natural in Stage 1, where every permit was issued; an authored deviation in Stage 2.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:108:  3. F3, wrong station: protection or a lent machine arrives at the wrong stake.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:109:  4. F4, wrong delivery: the pipe stencil does not match the purchase order (a P2 variant).
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:110:  5. F5, late arrival: a lent or ordered machine has not shown up when the plan says it has 
(the remobilization delay).
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:111:- **The argument.** At a conflict the foremen meet at the station and say why: the schedule 
put them a day apart, and a crane lays 180 ft a day while a loader backfills 250. Site listens and reports; Plan 
chooses between holding the crew and demobilizing it; Site confirms whichever happened.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:112:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:113:## The field manual
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:114:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:115:The manual is the workbook rewritten the way the Keep Talking manual is written. Site 
describes what it sees, the manual branches on that description plus the city, and nothing in it names the choice. Ben 
Kane's rule applies: an expert role that is only dense reading does not make good play, so every page ends in a 
question for another seat.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:116:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:117:1. **Front page, one rule.** Days lost to an event = rate drop × duration. The crew behind 
idles for whatever exceeds its buffer. Checked in simulation: late pipe (P2) at 50% for 3 days costs 1.5 days; a 1 day 
buffer idles backfill half a day, 1.5 days idles nothing.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:118:2. **City pages**, one per city: the Hazard Matrix row, the level signature, which situations 
can occur (Texas has no rock, so no E2; Las Vegas has no water or rain), and how responses change here (congested 
cities add 1 to 2 lead days and forbid sloping the trench; running sand halves a trench box's benefit and calls for 
shoring). The city's open-trench limit.
